[interviewer · STRUCTURE · 0:11] Understood. I'll come back to the cost breakdown for both years shortly. Before we pull data, look at your structure as a whole. Is it MECE, and what might it be missing?

[candidate · 0:19] Fair challenge. On revenue I'd say it's clean — stores times transactions per store times average ticket is multiplicative and exhaustive. On costs, "fixed versus variable" is MECE in principle but it's not very actionable; I'd rather cut it by line item: COGS (coffee beans, dairy, cups), labor, occupancy/rent, then corporate overhead, marketing, and D&A.

What I'm missing, I think, is three things. One, revenue mix — if the 15.0000% growth came from new stores that are dilutive, or from a shift toward lower-margin food versus espresso drinks, that shows up as margin decline without any cost line inflating. Two, price. Revenue up 15.0000% could be entirely volume with flat or discounted prices, which would mask a real unit-economics problem. Three, non-operating items — interest expense from debt on new builds, or one-time charges, sit below the line and hit net margin specifically.

That last one matters because the question says net margin, not gross or operating.
