[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 0:58] Understood. Beyond what we've discussed, what else could Brew & Bean do to reverse the margin decline? Give me as many distinct ideas as you can.

[candidate · 1:06] Beyond pricing, a few buckets.

On COGS directly: renegotiate or consolidate supplier contracts, since 200 locations is real buying power; hedge or forward-contract beans and dairy to stop the bleed; reformulate the highest-cost items — dairy especially, where oat and alternative milks may carry a premium that isn't being charged for; reduce waste and spoilage, which is pure COGS leakage; right-size portions and cup sizes.

On mix: push customers toward high-margin items. A straight espresso drink is mostly labor and a cheap input; food and specialty dairy drinks are where inflation hit. Loyalty programs and bundling can steer that.

On revenue beyond list price: reduce discounting, add a small surcharge on alternative milks, introduce premium tiers.

On overhead, the 2.0000 points: it grew 34.1667% against 15.0000% revenue growth, so there's roughly 19.1667 points of excess growth to examine — corporate headcount, marketing spend efficiency.

On labor: it's exactly variable at 15.0000%, so it's well-managed; I'd leave it alone rather than cut into service.

And close underperforming stores if any are dilutive.
