[candidate · 1:31] I'd compute a breakeven volume loss. Take the 10.0000% price case. Today, per index: revenue 115.0000, COGS 66.7000, labor 25.3000, overhead 16.1000, profit 6.9000. Contribution margin per unit today is (115.0000 − 66.7000 − 25.3000) ÷ 115.0000 = 23.0000 ÷ 115.0000 = 20.0000%.

Raise price 10.0000% with volume factor v: revenue = 126.5000v, COGS and labor scale with volume at 92.0000v, overhead fixed at 16.1000. Profit = 34.5000v − 16.1000. Set equal to today's 6.9000: 34.5000v = 23.0000, v = 0.6667. So traffic could fall 33.3333% before we're worse off than today.

That's an implied elasticity of 3.3333 — far above what specialty coffee typically shows, where loyalty is high and the absolute price change is maybe 50 cents on a $5.00 drink.

I'd change course if the pilot showed transaction decline past roughly 10.0000%, or if ticket size fell because customers traded down to cheaper drinks — that's margin loss the traffic number alone wouldn't catch.
